  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 AM EDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nEarly this morning, RAP analysis has the midlevel ridge axis \nshifting over the Great Lakes at this time, with the surface high \ncentered over the Lower Peninsula. Meanwhile, a shortwave is moving \ninto Manitoba and another into eastern Wyoming. With ridging over \nthe area, nighttime microphysics is showing mostly clear skies over \nthe UP, though some stray cirrus has been able to move in at times \namid the developing SW flow ahead of the Plains trough. Temperatures \nare falling back nicely into the lower 50s so far, apart from the \nfar west, where temperatures range in the upper 50s to lower 60s. We \nshould expect lows in the mid to upper 40s across most of the UP and \nthe lower to mid 50s in the far west. \n\nAs we head into the daytime hours, expect the midlevel ridge to \nbreak down, with flow aloft turning more zonal. The Wyoming \nshortwave moves eastward, with a surface low eventually closing off \nover the Plains. This looks to get \"scooped\" up in the broader \nmidlevel troughing that will be developing over the Plains late in \nthe day. Southerly return flow on the backside of the ridge will \nmake for another warm summer day with highs near 80 (a bit cooler in \nthe mid 70s near northern Lake Michigan). A cold front finally \nreaches the western UP late tonight, pressing into the eastern \n2/3rds by Saturday morning. Showers and a few thunderstorms are \nexpected along the frontal boundary, but with waning instability, no \nstrong to severe storms are anticipated. HREF QPF probs for only a \n0.10\" are nothing to write home about, only peaking at 50-70% in a \nstretch from Ironwood to Houghton. Eastward, light rain amounts a \ntenth of an inch or less is expected with showers dissipating past \nthe central UP.\n\n\nModest pressure rises and cold air advection will make for a windy \nbut dry day on Saturday. Expect an otherwise cooler day, as \ntemperatures climb from lows generally in the upper 50s to highs in \nthe 70s for most. Sunday, we can't rule out some scattered lake \neffect rain showers or at least clouds as 850 mb temps fall below 5C \n(0-2C if you're the Canadian or GFS) while the lake average surface \ntemp sits at ~17C.\n\nLooking into early next week, ensembles continue to hone in on a \ndeepening trough over the Rockies ejecting a Colorado Low in the \nvicinity of the Great Lakes. There remains considerable disagreement \nbetween each ensemble and their individual ensemble members \nregarding the track and strength of the surface low pressure. Given \na broad feature placement north and west of the UP, considerable \nmoisture (PWATS near 1.75-2\", the 95-99th percentile of \nclimatology), if the best QPF axis winds up in our neck of the woods \nthen meaningful rainfall could be expected. The NBM suggests a 60-\n80% chance for QPF >1.0\" by Tuesday morning, however, this may be \nhigh biased as individual ensembles and the LREF barely scrape 20-\n40%.",
